{"analysis":"Version 9 dropped to 84.5 from version 8's 85.4. The new rule that praising headlines are positive backfired.

It turned ads and promotional headlines into positive: 'Die besten Schweizer Kredite des Monats', 'Beste Preise ansehen', 'Magdeburger … Perfekter'. Neutral-to-positive errors rose from 10 to 17. Meanwhile it did not fix the target positives ('Das beste Buch des Jahres', 'Besucherzahlen explodieren', 'erfolgreich ihre Ausbildung' stayed neutral). Superlatives in headlines are mostly promotional in this data, so the version 8 boundary is better.

This is the last version, so I return to version 8's wording exactly. It is the best-scoring version and its remaining errors are scattered noise without a reliable new pattern.","question":"What sentiment does the author of this German tweet express: positive, neutral or negative?","rule":"Judge the author's own expressed feeling, not the topic. Headline-style posts with a link stay neutral even with critical slant, deaths or scandal, unless they use alarm wording or quote a victim's grief; ads, results, fan requests and calm remarks are neutral too, but the author's own pointed criticism of politicians is negative. Laughing, winking or heart cues make jokes, teasing, mild gripes and excited surprise positive, but do not soften stated pain or refusal. Bewilderment (o_O, wut), dry sarcasm ('Danke.', 'Aha!', 😑), curt rejections, threats and the author's bad state are negative.","criteria":{"positive":{"what":"Joy, praise, thanks, stand-alone greetings, welcomes, affection, excitement, pride in success, and jokes or teasing with laughing, winking or heart emoticons.","examples":["SIEG!!!!!!!! Chemie gewinnt 2-0 gegen Osnabrück :-D #liga3 (VICTORY!!! Chemie wins 2-0 against Osnabrück)","So unfassbar müde - gute Nacht, ihr Hübschen <3 (so incredibly tired - good night, you lovely people <3)","Schäm dich einfach :P (just be ashamed of yourself :P)"],"cues":":) / ;) / :D / :P / xD / haha / <3, Gruß (regards), willkommen (welcome), Glückwunsch (congratulations), danke (thanks)","not_for":"A smiley after heartbreak or refusal, e.g. 'Herz wieder in tausend Stücke zerfallen. Danke. (:' (heart shattered again. Thanks. (:), is negative. Ads and reported wins are neutral."},"neutral":{"what":"Headlines and news with links (also deaths, scandals, critical framing), sports results, ads, questions, likes, fan requests, mild everyday remarks and absurd jokes.","examples":["Selbstmord: US-Schauspieler Lee Thompson Young ist tot - Kultur | STERN.DE Mobile http (Suicide: US actor Lee Thompson Young is dead)","Trotz Teilverstaatlichung: Commerzbank-Chef Blessing fliegt First Class: Dank milliardenschwerer Staatshilfen ... http (Despite partial nationalization: Commerzbank CEO flies first class thanks to state aid)","Müdigkeit frisst Motivation #montag (tiredness eats motivation #monday)"],"cues":"headline plus link, RT of news, prices, Verkaufe (selling), +1, bitte spielt (please play), open questions","not_for":"Headlines with alarm wording, e.g. 'Alarmierende Studie' (alarming study), or a victim's grief are negative. Bewildered o_O reactions are negative."},"negative":{"what":"Complaints, anger, sadness, insults, threats, dry sarcasm, bewilderment, curt rejections, the author's own pointed political criticism, the author's bad state, alarm-worded news.","examples":["Och ne, jetzt singt Nicole auch noch? HILFE. #sg (Oh no, now Nicole is singing too? HELP.)","Einmal Weltuntergang zum Mitnehmen bitten. Danke. (One apocalypse to go, please. Thanks.)","Welches Geschenk hast du zuletzt bekommen? — Eine Bettdecke o.O http (What gift did you get last? — A duvet o.O)","RT @user: Alarmierende Studie: 68 Prozent aller Erstwähler können keinen Stift mehr bedienen ... http (Alarming study: 68 percent of first-time voters can no longer use a pen)"],"cues":":( / :/ / 😑 / o_O / T_T / wut, scheiße (shit), nervig (annoying), Mist (damn), merkwürdig (weird), einsam (lonely), Aha!, pssst, worst case","not_for":"Third-party criticism in headlines, e.g. 'Forrester-Analyst übt scharfe Kritik' (analyst sharply criticizes), stays neutral. Teasing with ;) and excited 'WTF O.O' are positive."}}}
